@Keycloak_HostAddress = http://localhost:8180
@Notifications_HostAddress = http://localhost:5106

### Start the AppHost first. Notifications has no public endpoints. It reacts to messages from the bus.
### The development endpoint runs a message through the same pipeline as a message from the bus, without a bus.
### It is on the port of the service, not on the gateway. The Authorization header becomes the token of the message.
### The answer says what the listener would do: Complete, Abandon or DeadLetter, with the reason.

### Login of alice (password: alice) => OK, with the access token for the requests below
# @name alice
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/cat-demo/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=cat-demo-cli&username=alice&password=alice

### List of the messages that Notifications receives
GET {{Notifications_HostAddress}}/dev/event-processor

### StockChanged with a token => Complete. The stock drops from 50 to 5, below the limit of 10, so the admin gets a mail (open Mailpit).
POST {{Notifications_HostAddress}}/dev/event-processor/StockChanged HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{alice.response.body.$.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isbn": "9780141036144",
  "warehouseId": 1,
  "oldQuantity": 50,
  "newQuantity": 5,
  "limit": 10
}

### StockChanged without a token => DeadLetter, Unauthorized
POST {{Notifications_HostAddress}}/dev/event-processor/StockChanged HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isbn": "9780141036144",
  "warehouseId": 1,
  "oldQuantity": 50,
  "newQuantity": 5,
  "limit": 10
}
